      "value": "**Project:** Zimmermann SFCC PWA Kit — Magento 2 → Salesforce Commerce Cloud **SI Partner:** Accenture **Architecture:** Slim overrides project on `@salesforce/retail-react-app` v10 (not a monorepo fork) **Period covered:** Pre-development setup phase, May 2026\n\nThe Approach: Human-in-the-Lead AI Development\n----------------------------------------------\n\nThis project was set up using a deliberate human-in-the-lead AI development model. That phrase describes something specific: **every architectural decision, every rule, and every governance boundary was made by a human.** AI was used to accelerate the execution of those decisions, propose options for the human to choose between, and implement approved plans — not to make design choices independently.\n\nThis distinction matters for leadership. The project is not \"AI-built\" — it is human-directed and AI-accelerated. The humans on this project defined what quality means, decided what was protected, chose the environment topology, and approved each implementation before it was committed.\n\nExecutive Summary\n-----------------\n\nBefore the first line of Zimmermann storefront code was written, a complete development infrastructure was designed and implemented through a structured collaboration between the project lead and two AI tools:\n\n**Stage 1 — Strategy and planning with Claude.ai.** Architecture decisions, environment strategies, and QA frameworks were discussed iteratively. Claude.ai proposed options with trade-offs; the project lead made the decisions. Outcomes were captured in planning documents (repo: `.docs/prompts/`) — these represent human decisions, not AI decisions.\n\n**Stage 2 — Review, correction and implementation with Claude Code CLI.** The planning documents were handed to Claude Code as implementation briefs. Claude Code first reviewed each document, identified errors and gaps specific to this project's structure, and flagged them for human review. After corrections were agreed, Claude Code implemented each item — always within a governance framework requiring human confirmation before significant changes.\n\n**What was built:** a production-ready development framework covering AI governance, quality gates, CI/CD pipelines, QA automation foundations, environment strategy, onboarding processes, branching rules, and team documentation — all in place before sprint 1 begins.\n\nPart 1 — The Planning Documents (Claude.ai Collaboration)\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\nStrategy documents created through Claude.ai conversations are stored in the repo at `.docs/prompts/` — the record of human decisions made during the planning phase, with Claude.ai acting as a knowledgeable thinking partner.\n\nEach planning document followed the same pattern: the project lead brought a problem or requirement to a Claude.ai session → Claude.ai proposed options, explained trade-offs, and asked clarifying questions one at a time, waiting for answers → the project lead made the decisions → Claude.ai drafted the document reflecting those decisions → the project lead reviewed, corrected, and approved the document → the approved document was handed to Claude Code as an implementation brief.\n\n**`.docs/prompts/qaClaudeCodeSetup.md` — QA Automation Framework.** The human decision: AI drafts tests, Lead QA validates them in plain English before any code is generated. Claude Code reviewed the planning document and caught implementation errors before they became committed code: the wrong test framework was referenced (corrected before implementation began), directory paths did not match the actual project structure (corrected), and the BrowserStack configuration needed to target only Zimmermann tests to avoid running the full upstream Salesforce suite.\n\nWhat was captured: Gherkin `.feature` files as source of truth (Lead QA must validate before Playwright specs are generated); Page Object Model with selectors living only in `e2e/support/pages/`; multi-region fixtures region-aware from day one (7 storefronts across 6 regions; US confirmed as a single site, REG-005); Magento baseline parity tests annotated `# DELTA:` on failure — never silently updated.\n\nThe Digital SIT quality gate and SIT environment strategy were defined in similar Claude.ai sessions — reflected in the `digital-sit.yml` CI workflow and the [05.6 - Environment Details](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/285540356) page.\n\nPart 2 — AI Governance Layer (AGENTS.md + .claude/)\n---------------------------------------------------\n\nThe most significant infrastructure investment was a comprehensive governance layer controlling precisely how Claude Code behaves on this project. Every rule was decided by a human and implemented by Claude Code under human direction. Central principle: **AI tools operate within explicit boundaries set by humans, not at their own discretion.**\n\n**AGENTS.md — the project rulebook** (with `CLAUDE.md` as a one-line stub). Every rule was a human decision — Claude.ai proposed options and explained trade-offs, the project lead chose. Contains: a Branch Rules table (which branches Claude Code can operate on and under what conditions); 14 Hard Rules — absolute prohibitions encoding team agreements (never modify design tokens, never rename shared hooks, never operate on protected branches, never use `--dangerously-skip-permissions` on shared branches, never reference later-sprint code); a Release Branch Behaviour section (supervised mode: state a plan, assess blast radius, wait for confirmation before every edit); a \"When to Stop and Ask a Human\" table; a Before-You-Run Checklist; and a Contact Map.\n\n**`.claude/rules/` — tiered file protection.** Tier 1 (Frozen, owner approval required before any change): design tokens (owner: UI Designer), Commerce SDK hooks (owner: Integration Lead), runtime config, CI/CD workflows. Tier 2 (Protected, second reviewer on PR required): global shell components, shared product components, shared commerce components, shared hooks with multiple consumers. Squad scope: even in squad-owned areas, only the owning squad should be prompting Claude Code there — Squad A owns checkout/cart/payments, Squad B owns PDP/PLP/search/account.\n\n**`.claude/hooks/` — automated checkpoints.** A branch-lock hook (in `.claude/settings.json`) physically blocks any Claude Code write when the current branch is `develop` or `main`. `tier2Warn.sh` surfaces a warning before editing a Tier 2 file. `contentfulDriftWarn.sh` checks for Contentful schema drift after a file edit. `testFileRemind.sh` reminds the developer to add a test/story after creating a new file.\n\n**`.claude/settings.json` — the permission model.** Reads freely (`git status`/`diff`/`log`, `find`, `grep`, `ls` — observation never requires approval); every write, edit, push, install, or destructive command requires approval; pre-hooks (branch lock + Tier 2 warning) and post-hooks (drift check + test reminder) fire automatically.\n\n**`.docs/memory/` — living project memory.** Five auto-maintained files give every Claude Code session current context before it acts: `architecture.md`, `projectState.md`, `feedback.md`, `references.md`, `userProfile.md`. Read at the start of every session via `/session-start`.\n\n**`.claude/skills/`** — human-directed workflow skills, invoked with a `/` prefix. Every skill was designed in collaboration with Claude.ai, reviewed by the project lead, and implemented by Claude Code. See the [Claude Code Skills Reference](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/297959425) page for the full reference — usage syntax, arguments, and each skill's human-in-the-lead checkpoint.\n\nPart 3 — Multi-IDE Support: Governance Independent of Tool Choice\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n`AGENTS.md` + `.claude/rules/` is the single source of truth for AI rules. A sync script propagates the same rules